::ENG1_U03_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School murals can make hallways feel more welcoming. Students who help paint murals often feel ownership of the space, and visitors can learn about school values from the images. Question: What is the central idea? {
=A. School murals can improve school spaces and communicate values.#Correct. This captures both the welcoming effect and the message murals can send.
~B. Paint comes in many colors.#Incorrect. This may be true, but it is not the main idea developed in the passage.
~C. Visitors always paint murals.#Incorrect. The passage says visitors can learn from murals, not that they paint them.
~D. Hallways should never have images.#Incorrect. The passage supports the value of hallway murals.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School murals can make hallways feel more welcoming. Students who help paint murals often feel ownership of the space, and visitors can learn about school values from the images. Question: Which detail best supports the central idea? {
~A. Hallways are part of school buildings.#Incorrect. This is true but too general to support the central idea about murals.
~B. Paintbrushes can be different sizes.#Incorrect. This detail is not in the passage and does not support the central idea.
=C. Students who help paint murals often feel ownership of the space.#Correct. This detail directly explains one positive effect of murals.
~D. Visitors may walk through buildings.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not explain the value of murals.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Drinking water during the day can support concentration. Even mild thirst can make it harder to focus, and water bottles at desks can remind students to drink before they feel tired. Question: Which evidence is most specific? {
=A. Mild thirst can make it harder to focus.#Correct. This evidence directly explains how hydration connects to concentration.
~B. Water exists in many places.#Incorrect. This is broad and does not support concentration.
~C. Students sometimes sit at desks.#Incorrect. This is a general fact and not strong support for the central idea.
~D. Bottles can be made of plastic or metal.#Incorrect. Bottle material is not connected to the central idea.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A school podcast can strengthen communication. Students plan topics, interview guests, edit audio, and publish episodes for the school community. Question: Which detail best supports the idea that podcasts build communication skills? {
~A. Podcasts can have titles.#Incorrect. Titles are related to podcasts but do not strongly show communication skill development.
~B. Schools often have communities.#Incorrect. This repeats a broad idea without showing skill development.
=C. Students plan topics and interview guests.#Correct. Planning topics and interviewing guests are clear communication tasks.
~D. Episodes can be published online.#Incorrect. Publication matters, but the strongest skill evidence is in the planning and interviewing.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Local history projects help students connect classroom learning to their community. Students may examine old photographs, read newspaper archives, and interview long-time residents. Question: What is the central idea? {
~A. Old photographs are always black and white.#Incorrect. This is not stated and is not the central idea.
~B. Newspapers should never be used for research.#Incorrect. The passage says students may read newspaper archives.
~C. Residents cannot help students learn.#Incorrect. Interviewing residents is given as a learning activity.
=D. Local history projects connect learning to community.#Correct. The passage develops this idea with examples of research activities.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Local history projects help students connect classroom learning to their community. Students may examine old photographs, read newspaper archives, and interview long-time residents. Question: Which detail is strongest support? {
~A. Students may use pencils.#Incorrect. This is not in the passage and does not support the central idea.
=B. Students interview long-time residents.#Correct. This directly connects learning to community voices and experiences.
~C. Classrooms have walls.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant to the central idea.
~D. History happened before today.#Incorrect. This is too broad and does not specifically support the project idea.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Outdoor learning can increase observation skills. During a nature walk, students record bird calls, compare leaf shapes, and notice how shade changes the temperature of the ground. Question: Which central idea is best supported? {
~A. Students should never learn indoors.#Incorrect. The passage supports outdoor learning but does not reject indoor learning.
=B. Outdoor learning helps students practice close observation.#Correct. The details show students noticing sounds, shapes, and temperature changes.
~C. Birds are the only important part of nature.#Incorrect. The passage includes birds, leaves, shade, and temperature.
~D. Shade always makes students stop learning.#Incorrect. Shade is part of what students observe, not a barrier to learning.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Outdoor learning can increase observation skills. During a nature walk, students record bird calls, compare leaf shapes, and notice how shade changes the temperature of the ground. Question: Why is the detail about comparing leaf shapes useful? {
~A. It explains an exact test score.#Incorrect. The passage does not discuss test scores.
~B. It proves leaves are more important than students.#Incorrect. The detail shows a learning activity, not a value judgment.
~C. It changes the passage into fiction.#Incorrect. The passage remains informational.
=D. It shows a specific observation activity.#Correct. Comparing leaf shapes is a concrete example of students practicing observation.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Peer review can help writers revise more effectively. A classmate may notice confusing sentences, missing evidence, or places where a transition would help the reader. Question: What is the central idea? {
~A. Writers should never revise their work.#Incorrect. The passage supports revision through peer review.
~B. Transitions are always unnecessary.#Incorrect. The passage says a transition may help the reader.
=C. Peer review can help writers improve drafts.#Correct. The details explain specific ways classmates can support revision.
~D. Classmates cannot notice writing problems.#Incorrect. The passage says classmates may notice several problems.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Peer review can help writers revise more effectively. A classmate may notice confusing sentences, missing evidence, or places where a transition would help the reader. Question: Which evidence best supports the central idea? {
=A. A classmate may notice confusing sentences.#Correct. This supports the idea because noticing confusion helps a writer revise.
~B. Writers use paper or computers.#Incorrect. This is not in the passage and does not explain how peer review helps.
~C. Some classrooms have desks.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to the central idea about revision support.
~D. Readers sometimes read silently.#Incorrect. This does not support the idea that peer review improves drafts.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School announcements can help students stay informed. Announcements may share club meetings, schedule changes, safety reminders, and celebration news. Question: Which detail would be weakest support? {
~A. Announcements may share schedule changes.#Incorrect. This directly supports staying informed.
~B. Announcements may share safety reminders.#Incorrect. Safety reminders are a useful type of information.
~C. Announcements may share club meetings.#Incorrect. Club meeting information supports the central idea.
=D. The announcement speaker is gray.#Correct. The speaker color does not explain how announcements help students stay informed.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School announcements can help students stay informed. Announcements may share club meetings, schedule changes, safety reminders, and celebration news. Question: Why is the evidence effective? {
~A. It gives no examples at all.#Incorrect. The passage gives examples including meetings, changes, reminders, and news.
~B. It is unrelated to the central idea.#Incorrect. The listed examples directly support the idea of staying informed.
=C. It gives several specific types of useful information.#Correct. The list shows exactly how announcements keep students informed.
~D. It proves announcements solve every school problem.#Incorrect. The evidence supports communication, not every possible problem.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Learning basic first aid can help students respond calmly in minor emergencies. Students can learn how to clean small cuts, call for adult help, and recognize when an injury needs more attention. Question: What is the central idea? {
~A. Students should handle every emergency alone.#Incorrect. The passage includes calling for adult help.
=B. Basic first aid can help students respond to minor emergencies.#Correct. The details explain specific first-aid responses.
~C. Small cuts are never important.#Incorrect. The passage says students can learn to clean small cuts.
~D. First aid is only about sports.#Incorrect. Sports are not mentioned.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Learning basic first aid can help students respond calmly in minor emergencies. Students can learn how to clean small cuts, call for adult help, and recognize when an injury needs more attention. Question: Which detail best supports calm response? {
=A. Students can recognize when an injury needs more attention.#Correct. This supports calm response because knowing when to seek more help can guide action.
~B. Emergencies never happen at school.#Incorrect. The passage discusses how students can respond to minor emergencies.
~C. First aid kits are sometimes red.#Incorrect. Kit color is not evidence for calm response.
~D. Students should ignore injuries.#Incorrect. The passage encourages appropriate response, not ignoring injuries.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A classroom debate can build listening skills. Students must hear opposing points, take notes, and respond to the actual claim instead of only repeating their own opinion. Question: Which central idea is best supported? {
~A. Debates require no listening.#Incorrect. The passage says listening is required.
=B. Classroom debates can strengthen listening skills.#Correct. The details focus on hearing opposing points and responding to claims.
~C. Opinions should never be spoken.#Incorrect. The passage does not reject opinions; it explains how to respond well.
~D. Notes are unrelated to debates.#Incorrect. Taking notes is one debate skill listed.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A classroom debate can build listening skills. Students must hear opposing points, take notes, and respond to the actual claim instead of only repeating their own opinion. Question: Why is "respond to the actual claim" strong evidence? {
~A. It proves students should memorize every word.#Incorrect. The passage emphasizes listening and response, not memorizing every word.
~B. It removes the need for notes.#Incorrect. The passage also lists taking notes as part of the process.
=C. It shows students are listening to what others say.#Correct. Responding to the actual claim requires understanding the other speaker.
~D. It changes debate into a story.#Incorrect. The passage remains informational.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School weather stations can make science more practical. Students collect temperature, wind, and rainfall data from their own campus, then compare patterns across weeks. Question: What is the central idea? {
~A. Weather never changes.#Incorrect. The passage discusses weather patterns across weeks.
~B. Campuses cannot collect data.#Incorrect. The passage says students collect data from campus.
~C. Rainfall is unrelated to weather.#Incorrect. Rainfall is one type of weather data.
=D. School weather stations can make science learning practical.#Correct. The details explain students collecting and comparing real campus data.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: School weather stations can make science more practical. Students collect temperature, wind, and rainfall data from their own campus, then compare patterns across weeks. Question: Which detail is most relevant? {
~A. Some students wear jackets.#Incorrect. This may relate to weather but is not evidence from the passage.
~B. The school has a front office.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to weather station learning.
=C. Students collect temperature, wind, and rainfall data.#Correct. This detail directly shows practical science work.
~D. Weeks have seven days.#Incorrect. This fact is too general and does not support the central idea.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A student newspaper can give students practice with real audiences. Writers choose topics classmates care about, check facts, and revise articles before publication. Question: What is the central idea? {
=A. A student newspaper helps writers practice for real audiences.#Correct. The details explain topic choice, fact checking, revision, and publication.
~B. Newspapers should avoid facts.#Incorrect. The passage says writers check facts.
~C. Students cannot choose topics.#Incorrect. The passage says writers choose topics classmates care about.
~D. Publication prevents revision.#Incorrect. The passage says writers revise before publication.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A student newspaper can give students practice with real audiences. Writers choose topics classmates care about, check facts, and revise articles before publication. Question: Which evidence best supports "real audiences"? {
~A. Articles use words.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not show audience awareness.
=B. Writers choose topics classmates care about.#Correct. This shows writers consider audience interests.
~C. Newspapers have pages.#Incorrect. Page format does not best support real audience practice.
~D. Students attend school.#Incorrect. This is background information, not evidence about audience.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Stretch breaks can help students reset during long study sessions. Standing, breathing deeply, or walking for two minutes can reduce stiffness and help students return to work with better focus. Question: Which central idea is best supported? {
~A. Students should never sit while studying.#Incorrect. The passage supports short breaks, not avoiding sitting entirely.
~B. Walking for two minutes is impossible.#Incorrect. The passage presents it as a possible break.
~C. Breathing is unrelated to focus.#Incorrect. Deep breathing is listed as a reset strategy.
=D. Short stretch breaks can help students return to studying with focus.#Correct. The passage explains brief actions that reduce stiffness and improve focus.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Stretch breaks can help students reset during long study sessions. Standing, breathing deeply, or walking for two minutes can reduce stiffness and help students return to work with better focus. Question: Which detail would be weakest support? {
=A. A pencil may be yellow.#Correct. Pencil color is unrelated to stretch breaks and focus.
~B. Walking for two minutes can reduce stiffness.#Incorrect. This directly supports the benefit of a stretch break.
~C. Breathing deeply can help students reset.#Incorrect. This supports the central idea by naming a reset strategy.
~D. Students can return to work with better focus.#Incorrect. This directly supports the central idea.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Local museums can make history easier to understand. Exhibits may show tools, letters, photographs, and recordings from people who lived through important events. Question: Why is the evidence strong? {
~A. It says museums have walls.#Incorrect. Walls do not explain how museums support understanding.
~B. It avoids all examples.#Incorrect. The passage gives several examples of exhibit materials.
=C. It lists specific exhibit materials that connect people to history.#Correct. The examples show how museums make history concrete.
~D. It proves every museum is identical.#Incorrect. The evidence describes possible exhibit items, not every museum.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Local museums can make history easier to understand. Exhibits may show tools, letters, photographs, and recordings from people who lived through important events. Question: What is the central idea? {
~A. Tools are the only useful objects in museums.#Incorrect. The passage also lists letters, photographs, and recordings.
~B. History should not be studied locally.#Incorrect. The passage supports local museums as learning resources.
~C. Recordings are always fictional.#Incorrect. The passage presents recordings as historical materials.
=D. Museums can make history easier to understand.#Correct. The evidence shows concrete ways museums present history.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Community sports leagues can teach teamwork. Players practice roles, communicate during games, and learn that one person's effort affects the entire group. Question: Which statement best evaluates the evidence? {
~A. The evidence is weak because it gives no examples.#Incorrect. The passage gives examples such as roles, communication, and group effort.
=B. The evidence is strong because it names specific teamwork behaviors.#Correct. The examples directly support how sports leagues teach teamwork.
~C. The evidence is unrelated because it discusses music.#Incorrect. The passage discusses sports leagues and teamwork, not music.
~D. The evidence proves teamwork is never difficult.#Incorrect. The passage supports teamwork learning, not the idea that it is always easy.
}
